# Reference verification, batch 8: s049-s054 (Nov 2026)
upd("n786", append="[Checked: Hilary, *De Trin.* (PL 10, archive.org OCR of Migne). Searches for *necessitas* near *Filius/nativitas/natura* found nothing usable, because the OCR of this volume is very poor. Scotus *Ord.* I d. 15 is not in the local Wadding text. Still open.]")
upd("n791", append="[Checked: *Phys.* I 9, 192a13–14 (First1KGreek text): ἡ μὲν γὰρ ὑπομένουσα συναιτία τῇ μορφῇ τῶν γιγνομένων ἐστίν (the matter that persists is a co-cause, with the form, of what comes to be). This is the nearest Aristotelian text. The maxim about the causes of *accidents* is not in Aristotle in this form. The *Auctoritates Aristotelis* were not consulted. Probable.]", status="probable")
upd("n794", append="[Checked: no Latin Dionysius (Eriugena, Sarracenus, or the *Corpus Dionysiacum*) was available on the box or opened in this batch. Still open.]")
upd("n800", append="[Checked: Wadding/Vivès *Ord.* I d. 30 qq. 1–2, ‘Utrum de Deo dicatur aliqua relatio ex tempore; Utrum Dei ad creaturam possit esse aliqua relatio realis’, matches the substance of s050-02/03. Gorris's preliminary conclusions on the visible mission run parallel to Bonaventure, *In I Sent.* d. 16 a. 1 q. 2 (‘Ad quid sit utilis missio visibilis Spiritus sancti’) and q. 3 (‘Quibus modis facta sit missio visibilis’), Quaracchi. *Ord.* I dd. 14–16 are not in the local Wadding text. Probable.]", status="probable")
upd("n801", text="The four visible species: the dove at the Baptism (Mt 3:16), the bright cloud at the Transfiguration (Mt 17:5), the breath (Jn 20:22, *insufflavit*), and the tongues of fire (Acts 2:3). Thomas, *ST* I q. 43 a. 7 ad 6, has the same list of four, but gives different purposes: the dove for the authority to give grace through regeneration, the cloud for the abundance of doctrine, the breath for the power of the ministry of the sacraments, and the tongues for the office of teaching. Gorris's purposes (humility, illumination of truth, fraternal charity, and preaching) are not Thomas's.", append="[Checked: *ST* I q. 43 a. 7 ad 6 (Corpus Thomisticum): ‘in Baptismo quidem sub specie columbae … ad ostendendum in Christo auctoritatem donandi gratiam … sub specie nubis lucidae, ad ostendendam exuberantiam doctrinae … sub specie flatus, ad ostendendam potestatem ministerii … sub linguis igneis, ad ostendendum officium doctrinae’. The earlier claim of ‘similar purposes’ has been corrected.]", status="verified")
upd("n802", append="[Checked: a search of the local Wadding/Vivès text of *Ord.* I d. 30 qq. 1–2 for *fundamental-* found no hit. The term has not been traced. Still open.]")
upd("n803", append="[Checked: the Wadding note to *Ord.* I d. 30 q. 2 cites ‘Henric. quodl. 7. quaest. 1. et quodl. 9. quaest. 4 [sic]. et quodl. 3. quaest. 4’, and the Scholium says ‘Ponit responsionem Henrici ad utramque quaestionem’. Henry, *Quodl.* IX q. 1 (Badius 1518) is ‘de relatione dei ad creaturas … Vtrum scilicet omnis relatio inter deum & creaturam sit quia ipsa creatura refertur ad deum: & non econuerso’. It says that God's relation to creatures arises through the essences known ‘ab intellectu diuino’ and ‘est in deo relatio secundum rationem ex parte voluntatis’. *Quodl.* IX q. 1 is the probable locus.]", status="probable")
upd("n804", append="[Checked: Lk 3:22 (Vulgate) is confirmed. The liturgical source was not searched. Still open.]")
upd("n805", append="[Checked: Augustine, *De Trin.* II 6.11 (augustinus.it) on the dove as a creature made for the purpose, quoted by Lombard, *Sent.* I d. 16 c. 1. One of the *aliqui* is very probably Bonaventure, *In I Sent.* d. 16 a. 1 q. 3 (Quaracchi): ‘nec columba fuit vera columba, quia statim desiit, sed similitudo columbae; similiter nec ignis verus ignis, quia non comburebat’. This matches Gorris's *quia non comburebat*. Probable.]", status="probable")
upd("n806", append="[Checked: Augustine, *De Trin.* II (augustinus.it Latin) has no *infallib-* there. *Ord.* I d. 16 is not in the local Wadding text. Still open.]")
upd("n812", append="[Checked: Wadding/Vivès heading, ‘Liber 1 > Distinctio 17 > Pars 1 > Quaestiones 1-2: Utrum necesse sit ponere caritatem creatam, formaliter inhaerentem naturae beatificabili’. Verified.]", status="verified")
upd("n815", append="[Checked: Henry, *Quodl.* IV q. 10 (Badius 1518): ‘Vtrum charitas re differat a gratia’. The topic fits. The Leuven edition was not consulted. Verified for the question number.]", status="verified")
upd("n817", append="[Checked: *De caelo* I 11 (First1KGreek): ὡς δέον ὁρίζεσθαι πρὸς τὸ τέλος καὶ τὴν ὑπεροχὴν τὴν δύναμιν, about 281a10–15. The line numbers are inferred, because the file lacks Bekker milestones. Verified for the chapter.]", status="verified")
upd("n821", text="Augustine, *De moribus ecclesiae catholicae* I 11.18–19 (PL 32, 1319), on Rom 8:35–39. ‘Quis nos separabit a caritate Christi’ is Rom 8:35, not Rom 4 as Gorris prints. The quotation goes back through Scotus, who has ‘Augustinus de Moribus Ecclesiae c. 4. pertractans illud ad Rom. 8 … Charitas, inquit, Dei hic dicta est virtus, quae animi nostri rectissima est affectio’ (*Ord.* I d. 17 p. 1, Wadding/Vivès). Augustine's own words are ‘Sin illa virtus dicta est, quae ipsius animi nostri rectissima affectio est’ (I 11.19). Compare I 15.25: ‘nihil omnino esse virtutem affirmaverim nisi summum amorem Dei’.", append="[Checked: augustinus.it Latin text of *De moribus* I, and Wadding/Vivès d. 17 p. 1. Gorris's ‘c. 4’ is Scotus's chapter number, and the passage is I 11.19. Verified, with correction.]", status="verified")
upd("n824", append="[Checked: *Ord.* I d. 17 p. 1 qq. 1–2 (Wadding/Vivès) has the acceptatio doctrine (‘quo habens formaliter acceptetur a Deo, et quo actus elicitus ejus acceptetur tanquam meritorius’). Verified.]", status="verified")
upd("n828", append="[Checked: the Wadding Scholia to *Ord.* I d. 17 p. 1 q. 2: ‘affert primam sententiam, quae est Henrici dicentis, habitum acquisitum dare expeditionem … habitum vero infusum …’; ‘Secunda sententia est Goffredi dicentis substantiam actus fieri a potentia, intensionem tamen ab habitu’. Question title: ‘Utrum habitus sit principium activum respectu actus?’. Verified.]", status="verified")
upd("n830", append="[Checked: 2 Mcc 15:12–14 (Vulgate): ‘Hic est fratrum amator, et populi Israel … Jeremias propheta Dei’ (v. 14). Verified.]", status="verified")
upd("n831", append="[Checked: Scotus, *Ord.* I d. 17 p. 1 q. 2 (Wadding/Vivès): ‘Habitus est, quod habens utitur cum voluerit, secundum Commentatorem 3. de Anima, super illud: Oportet ut in ea sit intellectus’. Thomas, *ST* I-II q. 49 a. 3 s.c., has ‘Commentator dicit, in III de anima, quod habitus est quo quis agit cum voluerit’. The Latin Averroes was not opened in this batch, so the comment number 18 is not confirmed. Probable.]", status="probable")
upd("n832", append="[Checked: Wadding/Vivès, *Ord.* I d. 17 p. 1 q. 2: ‘Tertio modo … potest dici quod habitus est causa partialis activa cum quo ipsa potentia etiam est causa partialis respectu actus perfecti’. The Scholium has ‘Tertia sententia tribuit habitui causalitatem partialem, et haec videtur Doctoris’. A later Scholium, ‘Sententiam proxime positam, quae videtur propria, impugnat quinque rationibus’, shows that Scotus also argues against it. That fits Gorris's cautious *sustentabilis*. Gorris's list counts it as the fourth opinion and Scotus's as the third *modus*. Verified.]", status="verified")
upd("n833", append="[Checked: *Ord.* I d. 17 p. 1 q. 2 (Wadding/Vivès). No named bearer of the ‘inclination only’ view was found. Scotus discusses the habit that *inclinat potentiam*, but in his own argument. Still open.]")
upd("n835", append="[Checked: *ST* I-II q. 50 a. 5 co. (Corpus Thomisticum): ‘oportet in voluntate aliquem habitum ponere, quo bene disponatur ad suum actum’. Compare q. 49 a. 3 and a. 4. This is the closest text to Gorris's *ad bene recipiendum actus*. Probable.]", status="probable")
upd("n836", append="[Checked: *Ord.* I d. 17 p. 1 (Wadding/Vivès): ‘quo habens formaliter acceptetur a Deo, et quo actus elicitus ejus acceptetur tanquam meritorius’. Verified.]", status="verified")
upd("n838", append="[Checked: Bonaventure, *In I Sent.* d. 17 p. 1 a. 1 q. 3 (Quaracchi), ‘Utrum quis certitudinaliter scire possit, se esse in caritate’, is the standard parallel question. The OCR text has no argument from the sacraments. The local Wadding *Ord.* I d. 17 p. 1 has no matching passage either. The *quidam* has not been identified. Still open.]")
upd("n842", append="[Checked: Wadding/Vivès, ‘Liber 1 > Distinctio 17 > Pars 2 > Quaestio 1: Utrum tota caritas praeexsistens corrumpatur ita quod nulla realitas eadem numero maneat in caritate maiore et minore’. Verified.]", status="verified")
upd("n844", append="[Checked: Godfrey, *Quodl.* XI q. 3 (Hoffmans OCR): ‘Utrum ad hoc quod quantitas remanens possit transmutari oporteat praeter quantitatem determinatam ponere aliam quantitatem indeterminatam’. It treats increase and decrease of quantity and quality, also in the Eucharistic species, and says ‘maior quantitas est alia a minori secundum rem’. That fits both the corruption thesis and Scotus's sixth argument. Verified. *Quodl.* VII q. 7 (SCTA), ‘Utrum essentia angeli sit composita ex genere et differentia’, puts species in distinct *gradus naturae*. That fits the confirmation ‘sicut in pluribus speciebus’ and is probable. IX q. 4 could not be checked, because it is not available locally. Note also Godfrey's direct treatments of augmentation, *Quodl.* II q. 10 (‘Utrum caritas sive quicumque habitus possit augeri per essentiam’) and VII q. 12 (‘Utrum caritas possit augeri in infinitum’), which Gorris does not cite. Probable overall.]", status="probable")
upd("n845", append="[Checked: Wadding Scholium to *Ord.* I d. 17 p. 2 q. 1: ‘Refellit hanc opinionem … sex viis’. The sixth is the argument from the rarefaction of the species of wine. The note to the question adds ‘omnes contra Goffred.’. Verified.]", status="verified")
upd("n846", append="[Checked: *De gen. et corr.* I 5, 320a (First1KGreek): ἡ μὲν … γένεσίς ἐστιν, ἡ δὲ περὶ μέγεθος αὔξησις. Growth concerns magnitude and requires that what grows persists. Compare Scotus, ‘primo de Generatione, auctum oportet manere’ (d. 17 p. 2 q. 1). Verified.]", status="verified")
upd("n847", text="Thomas Aquinas, *ST* II-II q. 24 aa. 4–5. Charity increases in its essence, and for Thomas this is the same as being more in the subject: ‘nihil est aliud ipsam secundum essentiam augeri quam eam magis inesse subiecto, quod est eam magis radicari in subiecto’ (a. 4 ad 3). It does not increase by the addition of charity to charity (a. 5). The following question (s054) cites the parallel *ST* I-II q. 52 aa. 1–2.", append="[Checked: Corpus Thomisticum II-II q. 24. In a. 4 ad 3 Thomas rejects those who say charity grows ‘non … secundum suam essentiam, sed solum secundum radicationem’ (‘hi propriam vocem ignoraverunt’), and identifies essential increase with *magis radicari*. The earlier Latin tag *secundum maiorem radicationem in subiecto* was not Thomas's wording and has been replaced.]", status="verified")
upd("n849", append="[Checked: *ST* I-II q. 52 a. 2 co.: the increase ‘non fit per additionem formae ad formam; sed fit per hoc quod subiectum magis vel minus perfecte participat unam et eandem formam’. So for Thomas the form remains and is not corrupted. Wadding attributes the corruption thesis to Godfrey (‘Ponitur sententia Goffredi dicentis totam entitatem praeexistentem in augmentatione deperdi’). Verified.]", status="verified")
upd("n850", text="The Eucharistic argument is Scotus's own, in *Ord.* I d. 17 p. 2 q. 1. When the species of wine are rarefied, the quantity becomes greater. If the greater were a new individual, the quantity that belonged to the wine would not remain, and so neither would the blood, which is held to remain only as long as the accidents that affected the wine remain. Godfrey discusses the same case in *Quodl.* XI q. 3. Compare also Scotus, *Ord.* IV d. 12.", append="[Checked: Wadding/Vivès d. 17 p. 2 q. 1: ‘si rarefiant species vini in sacramento altaris, erit quantitas molis major quam prius … ergo illa quantitas, quae fuit vini, non manet post rarefactionem; igitur nec manet ibi sanguis’. Gorris writes *eucaristia* generally. *Ord.* IV d. 12 was not found in the local text. Verified for d. 17.]", status="verified")
upd("n855", append="[Checked: Wadding/Vivès, ‘Liber 1 > Distinctio 17 > Pars 2 > Quaestio 2: Utrum illud positivum caritatis praeexsistentis quod manet in augmento, sit tota essentia caritatis intensae’. Verified.]", status="verified")
upd("n858", append="[Checked: Giles of Rome, *Quodl.* (Louvain 1646) is not available locally or in the SCTA transcription. Only I q. 6 is. Scotus (Wadding/Vivès d. 17 p. 2 q. 2) does have the lemmas ‘Alia via qua dicit de indispositione, sive de amotione contrarii’ and ‘Tertia etiam via, quae tenetur a quibusdam de radicatione formae in subjecto’. Still open for Giles.]")
upd("n859", append="[Checked: Henry, *Quodl.* XIV q. 3 (Badius 1518): ‘vtrum forma separata: puta color aut albedo habeat omnem rationem illorum quae sub sua specie habent participari in subiecto’. It treats the intensio and remissio of accidental forms ‘secundum gradus perfectionis in essentia earum’ and says that a form ‘habens contrarium in materia, per cuius admixtionem obumbratur’. That is relevant to the *amotio contrarii* lemma. Probable.]", status="probable")
upd("n862", append="[Checked: Scotus d. 17 p. 2 q. 2 (Wadding/Vivès) has the three viae: dispositio, amotio contrarii, and radicatio ‘a quibusdam’. Thomas, II-II q. 24 a. 4 ad 3, identifies essential increase with *magis radicari in subiecto*. Giles was not checked. Probable.]", status="probable")
upd("n863", text="*Metaph.* VIII 3, 1043b32–1044a11. The definition is a kind of number (ὁ ὁρισμὸς ἀριθμός τις), and like number it is changed by any addition or subtraction (1043b33–1044a2). Just as number does not admit of more and less, neither does substance in respect of form (1044a9–11).", append="[Checked: Perseus Greek with Bekker milestones. The earlier note gave only 1044a9–11 and paraphrased it as ‘the definition is indivisible’. The passage has been refined.]", status="verified")
upd("n864", append="[Checked: *Eth. Nic.* (Perseus) II 1, 1103b6–21 (ἐκ τῶν αὐτῶν … γίνεται πᾶσα ἀρετὴ καὶ φθείρεται; ἐκ τῶν ὁμοίων ἐνεργειῶν αἱ ἕξεις γίνονται), II 2, 1104a11–27, and III 5, 1114a4–10. For loss through *cessation* of acts, the usual text is VIII 5, 1157b10–13 (πολλὰς δὴ φιλίας ἀπροσηγορία διέλυσεν). Thomas cites that text on this very point, II-II q. 24 a. 10 co.: ‘Per cessationem quidem ab actu diminuuntur virtutes ex actibus acquisitae … multas amicitias inappellatio solvit’. Gorris's ‘2° et 3°’ remain approximate. Probable.]", status="probable")
upd("n865", append="[Checked: *ST* II-II q. 24 a. 10 co. (Corpus Thomisticum): ‘nec per peccatum veniale caritas diminui potest, neque effective, neque meritorie’, and mortal sin destroys charity. Verified.]", status="verified")
